<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#waqf">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Waqf</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as ḥabs, habous, vakıf, awqāf</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A waqf, also called a ḥabs, is an irrevocable trust in Islamic law, usually an endowment of real estate or other assets whose revenues serve a specified purpose, such as a mosque, school, fountain or the founder's family. Once declared, the property cannot be sold, transferred or given away and is legally held to belong to God; the founder appoints a trustee to manage it. The practice rests on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadiths</a>, among them Umar's endowment of land at Khaybar on Muhammad's advice, but the formal system emerged from the 9th century: the oldest dated deed, of 876 CE, concerns a Qur'an now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> Waqf manages the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-aqsa-mosque">al-Aqsa Mosque</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>There is no direct Quranic injunction about awqāf, but one is inferred from Surah Al Imran (3:92), which says that righteousness is not reached until a person gives some of what they cherish. The institution rests on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadiths</a>. One tradition says that after the Hijrah the first waqf was a grove of 600 date palms whose proceeds fed the poor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>.</p><p>The two oldest known deeds, or waqfiyas, are from the 9th century and a third from the early 10th, all within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> the earliest known waqf was a pond called Birkat Ḥabash with its orchards, founded in 919 to run a hydraulic complex and feed the poor. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> the 14th-century Insha-i-Mahru records that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-of-ghor">Muhammad of Ghor</a> gave two villages to a congregational mosque in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multan">Multan</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>A waqf is a contract, so the founder, the waqif, must be an adult of sound mind who can handle financial affairs. Being a Muslim is not required, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhimmi">dhimmis</a> may found one. The founder must name a specific beneficiary, may not reserve a right to sell or revoke, and must own the property outright. The founder appoints an administrator, a nāẓir or mutawallī, and sets the rules for successors.</p><p>A waqf is meant to last forever, but Islamic law admits conditions under which it ends: if its goods are destroyed or no longer used as the founder intended, or if a judge, the ḳāḍī, declares it void for an illegal act or purpose. It is also void if the founder converts to another religion.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Waqf revenue paid for mosques, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a>, public kitchens, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">caravanserais</a> and libraries. After waqf law and madrasa foundations were firmly established by the 10th century, the number of hospitals multiplied, and by the 11th century many Islamic cities had several. Waqfs paid doctors, surgeons and pharmacists, bought food, medicines and equipment, kept buildings in repair and supported medical schools. The institution was not popular everywhere: in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> very few examples are found, and they were limited to the area around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djenne">Djenné</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Waqf” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Samuel appointed Mohammad Amin al-Husseini Grand Mufti of Jerusalem in 1921, and in 1922 he became president of the Supreme Muslim Council, controlling the <b>Waqf</b> and the Islamic courts; the rivalry of his clan with the Nashashibis dominated Palestinian politics.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mandatory-palestine">Mandatory Palestine · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…guardians of proper Islamic government, restored Maliki Sunnism as the official religion after Almohadism, allied with the Maliki ulama of the cities and with the sharifian families, with whom they sometimes intermarried, and in Fez insisted on appointing the officials of religious institutions and managing the <b>waqf</b> endowments.</blockquote><a href="/empire/marinid-sultanate">Marinid Sultanate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Religious law was administered by qadis following the Hanafi school, while charitable endowments (<b>waqfs</b>) financed mosques, madrasas, hospitals and caravanserais.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…built institutions that served religious and public life: the madrasa, the college of religious learning, which spread from the 11th century; the <b>waqf</b>, the charitable endowment that funded mosques, schools, hospitals, fountains and caravanserais; courts led by judges, the qadis; and Sufi lodges that offered hospitality and teaching.</blockquote><a href="/religion/islam">Islam · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>The Dome is maintained by the Ministry of <b>Awqaf</b> in Amman.</blockquote><a href="/work/dome-of-the-rock">Dome of the Rock · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The documents of the mosque's <b>waqf</b> (religious endowment) explicitly claim that ostentatious ornamentation of gold or jewels was avoided in order to conform with the traditions of the Islamic prophet Muhammad.</blockquote><a href="/work/suleymaniye-mosque">Süleymaniye Mosque · What It Is</a></li></ul></section>
